    "q-100790": {
      "dom_id": "step-q100790",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the three sequences below are arithmetic sequences?\n\nI. $-2,\\,-4,\\,-6,\\,-8,...$\n\nII. $-2,\\,-9,\\,-16,\\,-23,...$\n\nIII. $-2,\\,4,\\,10,\\,16,...$",
      "worked_solution": "To determine whether each sequence is an arithmetic sequence, we need to look at the jumps between terms in the sequence. If the jumps are the same, then they indeed represent a common difference of an arithmetic sequence. \n\n- In sequence I, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence I is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14203588/assets/c599604e6630b0dbf74d646f93b6330b5d310f35bfbce7b008f16d1686534c77.png)\n\n- In sequence II, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence II is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=-7.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14203588/assets/b35cc27bfa6f7fe2171529a0c84e9ab8d33c122e932512fae1b06fe21cc9bc94.png)\n\n- In sequence III, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence III is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14203588/assets/9aa43107c2b5c975450590f4bc126273b45ddc72f1acfd3d35eadff0313ded1b.png)\n\nIn conclusion, sequences I, II, and III are arithmetic sequences.",
